```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, state, parent=None, action=None):
        self.state = state
        self.parent = parent
        self.action = action

    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Node({self.state})"
```

`__init__` is the initialiser, invoked automatically by `Node("A")`. `self` is the instance and is always the explicit first parameter of an instance method — Python declines to hide it. Attributes are created simply by assigning to `self.something`; there is no separate declaration section.

```python
class StackFrontier:
    def remove(self):
        return self.frontier.pop()

class QueueFrontier(StackFrontier):
    def remove(self):
        return self.frontier.pop(0)
```

The subclass inherits everything it does not override. `QueueFrontier` differs from its parent in exactly one method, and that single difference converts depth-first search into breadth-first search — the cleanest illustration in the whole course of why the two algorithms are the same algorithm.

Call the parent implementation with `super()`:

```python
class Timed(StackFrontier):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.calls = 0
```

An assignment in the class body is shared by all instances; an assignment to `self` is per-instance. A mutable class attribute is the class-level version of the mutable default argument from **Python Functions**, and fails the same way.

```python
class Bad:
    seen = []            # shared by every instance

class Good:
    def __init__(self):
        self.seen = []   # one per instance
```

Every search in the course is this loop, differing only in how the next node is chosen.

```python
frontier = deque([start])
parent = {start: None}
while frontier:
    node = frontier.popleft()          # pop() would make it depth-first
    if node == goal:
        return build_path(parent, goal)
    for nxt in neighbours(node):
        if nxt not in parent:
            parent[nxt] = node
            frontier.append(nxt)
return None
```

The `parent` dict does double duty: it records how each state was reached *and* serves as the explored set, since membership in it means "already discovered".

> [!warning] Mark on enqueue, not on dequeue
> If a node is only marked explored when it is removed from the frontier, a cyclic or densely connected graph will enqueue the same state many times before any of the copies is processed. In Degrees, with actors sharing dozens of films, that alone is the difference between a query returning and a query appearing to hang.

Minimax is the same recursion pattern, with the value of a state defined in terms of the values of its successors and a base case at terminal states.

```python
def minimax_value(state):
    if terminal(state):
        return utility(state)
    if player(state) == MAX:
        return max(minimax_value(result(state, a)) for a in actions(state))
    return min(minimax_value(result(state, a)) for a in actions(state))
```

Note the dependency on lesson 5: `result` must return a fresh board, or the recursion corrupts the state it is exploring.
